# Who owns what.
#
# This file does two jobs at once:
#
#   1. It requests a review automatically when a pull request is marked ready
#      for review. It does not fire on drafts, which is the point: open a draft
#      while you work, mark it ready when you want eyes on it.
#
#   2. It is the ownership map. Check who is listed for the area you are about
#      to touch, before you start. Two people writing the same module in
#      parallel is the most expensive failure in concurrent work: nobody finds
#      out until the merge, and then finished, working code has to be thrown
#      away.
#
# One rule this file must keep: no path may have a single owner. A code owner
# is never asked to review their own pull request, so a path owned by one
# person requests nothing and the file becomes decorative. Every line below
# lists at least two people for that reason.
#
# The split below is a starting point based on where each area has been worked.
# Adjust it to match reality - just keep every line at two names or more.

# Fallback: anything not matched by a rule below.
*                   @jikaidoko @THAPOVS @Myebou

# Circuits, their tests and the off-chain drivers.
/contracts/         @jikaidoko @Myebou

# Interface, bundler configuration and the browser runtime.
/frontend/          @THAPOVS @jikaidoko

# Repository tooling.
/scripts/           @jikaidoko @THAPOVS

# Automation and process. Changes here affect everyone.
/.github/           @jikaidoko @THAPOVS @Myebou
